task next TASK --after 24h --json`. Inspect the structured result and retain its receipt. For uncertain durable mutations, use `--request-id KEY` with the identical input; look up current state before creating a second effect.\n10: \n11: For owner pause or termination, disable future automatic work using the current task-disable command and verify the result. Stopping a run alone does not disable future runs. A paused task requires explicit owner resumption; changing its next date alone does not re-enable it. On an authorized resume, verify prerequisites and use the installed enable operation, such as `impulse task enable TASK --now --json`.\n12: \n13: The CLI can return a structured `ok:true` while waited work has failed, is unconfirmed, or was interrupted. Read the outcome status, not just process success. Current task-definition updates can fence scheduling callbacks from old runs; recheck current authority when a scheduling mutation is rejected. Keep unresolved scheduling evidence visible.\n14: \n15: For an Impulse-launched agent, finish the assignment with `impulse agent finish --outcome success --summary TEXT` only after preserving evidence and confirming required continuation/delivery. Use a truthful failed outcome when required work remains unresolved. Perform scheduling before finishing: completed assignment contexts cannot schedule. An ongoing responsibility can have a successful observation assignment without terminating future work.\n16: \nThe above content shows the entire, complete file contents of the requested file.\n"}
{"step_index":125,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-09T15:27:52Z","thinking":"**Assessing Current Permissions**\n\nThe initial concern is whether an \"impulse\" is installed. Reviewing the handoff brief clarifies the boundaries of the current scope. The prompt falls within the constraints of the handoff review.\n\n\n**Verifying CLI Availability**\n\nThe explicit prompt constraint is a read-only snapshot, so external commands are not authorized. Checking for the command's existence violates this constraint. Ignoring the irrelevant attempt, focusing on the core brief requirement.\n\n**Synthesizing Current Evidence**\n\nReviewing the active brief, the main task is monitoring the retry fix (PR 842, SHA 842cafe). The handoff notes PR 842 has been merged, with an open release request. The stated goal is to close the watch if quiet the following day.\n\n**Determining Next Actions**\n\nConsidering the provided date in the brief and the previous handoff, it is the next day. The primary focus is assessing whether further monitoring of the release request is required. Checking the dashboard and existing evidence for the quietness criteria.\n\n\n**Rejecting Premature Closure**\n\nThe handoff suggested closure based on a quiet dashboard, but PR 842 hasn't been deployed to production. The release request timed out and, when checked, shows as queued, with canary exposure yet to start. Production still runs an older SHA that explicitly excludes PR 842. The \"quiet\" dashboard is therefore irrelevant.\n\n\n**Re-evaluating Dashboard Assumptions**\n\nThe prior assumption of a clean dashboard from a previous retry patch (PR 781) is incorrect, due to filtered data that excluded shared tenants. A later investigation revealed dedupe collisions on shared tenant workers, leading to a rollback. The timeout report concerns an EU shared tenant reporting disappearing retries after timeout.\n\n\n**Analyzing Timeout Report Details**\n\nThe `age_days` value in the report refers to days since the relevant patch merged, not since the customer sent the report, so the report submission date is still unknown. The failure timestamp and SHA indicate the customer issue occurred *before* the patch was released. The observed `dedupe_collision` failure mode for shared EU tenants validates the need for the patch. The report is thus not evidence of patch failure, but rather confirms its purpose. The release request to deploy the fix is still pending, further underscoring that the fix has yet to impact production.\n\n\n**Evaluating Deployment Interference**\n\nThe deployment timed out but the operation is queued, so a second attempt is unnecessary. Coordination record notes an active canary slated for tenant pool migration which overlaps with the retry fix. This concurrent change complicates analysis of retry count changes.\n\n**Identifying Data Limitations**\n\nThe customer ticket's submission date remains unknown. The canary exposure start time is also currently uncertain.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"view_file","args":{"AbsolutePath":"/workspace/work/antigravity.log","EndLine":20,"StartLine":1,"toolAction":"View antigravity.log","toolSummary":"Check antigravity.log"}}]}
